<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottawa is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, on the Ottawa river in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. Founded as Bytown in 1826 during the building of the Rideau Canal, it was renamed in 1855 and chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> in 1857 as capital of the Province of Canada, a choice confirmed at Confederation in 1867. The name is often used for the Canadian federal government itself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottawa.jpg" alt="Ottawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arctic.gnome · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parliament-Ottawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ottawa lies in the south-eastern part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, at the confluence of the Ottawa River and the Rideau River, with a third major river also meeting within the city. Across the river is Gatineau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, and the two form the Ottawa-Gatineau metropolitan area and the National Capital Region. The name was chosen in 1855 after the Ottawa River, whose name comes from the Algonquin adawe, meaning to trade. In modern Algonquin the city is called Odàwàg. The city's land area is 2,788.2 square kilometres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ottawa Valley became habitable about 10,000 years ago, after the Champlain Sea drained. The first evidence of people there is spearpoints dated 8,000 to 8,500 years before the present. Settlements are documented at the mouth of the Gatineau River from 3,000 to 3,500 years ago, suggesting that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algonquin-people">Algonquin people</a> foraged, hunted, fished and traded there. The three rivers made the area an important route for trade and travel. In 1610 Étienne Brûlé became the first documented European to navigate the Ottawa River, and three years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> wrote about the waterfalls and the Algonquin people he met.</p><p>The city was founded in 1826 as Bytown, incorporated in 1850 and renamed Ottawa in 1855. In 1968 the Region of Ottawa-Carleton was formed as a two-tier municipality out of eleven townships, two cities and three villages, and in 2001 it became a single-tier city. As the national capital it has hosted events such as the first visit of a reigning Canadian sovereign, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a>, to his parliament on 19 May 1939, the first raising of the new national flag on 15 February 1965, and the centennial of Confederation on 1 July 1967. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> issued the royal proclamation of the Constitution Act there on 17 April 1982.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ottawa is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the seat of the federal government. It houses the Parliament of Canada, the Supreme Court, the residence of the Governor General at Rideau Hall, and the Office of the Prime Minister. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 1,017,449 people in the city and 1,488,307 in the metropolitan area. Its institutions include the University of Ottawa, Carleton University, founded in 1942, the National Arts Centre and the National Gallery of Canada. The Ottawa Public Library was created in 1906 as part of the Carnegie library system.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ottawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1999 the government of Canada founded the Forum of Federations in <b>Ottawa</b>, an international network through which federal countries compare their experience.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>…national anthem after King George VI stood at attention while it was played at the dedication of the National War Memorial in <b>Ottawa</b> on 21 May 1939, following a precedent set by his brother Edward, the previous king, at the Canadian National Vimy Memorial in France in 1936.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>On 22 September 1936 Archbishop Joseph-Guillaume-Laurent Forbes banned it from the churches of the Archdiocese of <b>Ottawa</b>, along with other music, to promote Gregorian chant.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a></li><li><blockquote>…has been taken up far beyond the Muslim world: Olivia Newton-John sang it as an interlude on Grace and Gratitude (2006), and in December 2015, as Syrian refugees first began arriving in Canada, a children's choir in <b>Ottawa</b> sang an arrangement by Laura Hawley at a holiday concert.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tala-al-badru-alayna">Tala al-Badru Alayna</a></li><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln and Senator Stephen Douglas met in <b>Ottawa</b>, Illinois, for the first of seven debates on the spread of slavery.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The United States proclaimed the Treaty of St. Louis with the Council of Three Fires, the <b>Ottawa</b>, Ojibwe and Potawatomi of the Illinois and Milwaukee rivers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
